  "narrative": {
    "intro": "FY2026 to date shows $4B in federal contract obligations to Mississippi. The state recorded 73,777 awards at an average award size of $54.3K, and obligations fell 49.9% year over year. The Department of Defense obligated $3.3B, making the DOD the dominant buyer in the state. Ship Building and Repairing accounted for $2.2B in obligations, and HUNTINGTON INGALLS INCORPORATED received $2.2B in awards. Other notable buyers include the National Aeronautics and Space Administration with $235M and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $143M. Other industry concentrations include Other Support Activities for Air Transportation at $472M and Facilities Support Services at $132M. Figures are contract obligations. Current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date). This summary highlights where obligations are concentrated by agency, vendor, and industry, and provides a snapshot of how FY2026 to date compares to prior fiscal years, where obligations ranged from $4.9B to $8.7B in the FY21–FY25 period.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated are federal obligations in Mississippi and which buyers dominate?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $3.3B in FY2026 to date. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration and the Department of Veterans Affairs follow with $235M and $143M respectively. Concentration is high in Ship Building and Repairing, which received $2.2B, indicating a narrow set of buyers and industries account for most obligations."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the largest contractors and which industries lead in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "HUNTINGTON INGALLS INCORPORATED is the largest contractor with $2.2B in obligations. V2X AEROSPACE LLC and M1 SUPPORT SERVICES, L.P. follow with $420M and $86.3M respectively. Ship Building and Repairing leads industries at $2.2B, followed by Other Support Activities for Air Transportation at $472M and Facilities Support Services at $132M."
      }
    ]
  },
